WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.014 --> 00:00:10.835
In a laboratory in Japan, a scientist examines an ancient block of ice.

2
00:00:11.735 --> 00:00:17.317
It is hard to distinguish at first glance, but the scientist can make out a long, curved tusk

3
00:00:17.597 --> 00:00:18.477
encased within it.

4
00:00:19.258 --> 00:00:25.339
Inside this mass of ice is the frozen carcass of a baby woolly mammoth that was discovered in

5
00:00:25.379 --> 00:00:26.780
the tundra of Siberia.

6
00:00:27.464 --> 00:00:34.488
With great care the scientist slowly removes ice from the body, hoping to find intact DNA.

7
00:00:35.129 --> 00:00:38.031
The woolly mammoth is an extinct relative of the elephant.

8
00:00:38.751 --> 00:00:44.655
Even though a common ancestor lived many, many thousands of years ago, the elephant is not a direct

9
00:00:44.695 --> 00:00:45.995
descendant of the mammoth.

10
00:00:46.776 --> 00:00:53.080
In fact, the species diverged into separate evolutionary branches as long as six million years ago.

11
00:00:53.844 --> 00:00:58.668
The woolly mammoth first appeared only two million years ago, during the Pleistocene epoch.

12
00:00:59.328 --> 00:01:04.852
They survived until as recently as ten thousand years ago, when the last ice age ended.

13
00:01:05.612 --> 00:01:09.735
You could say that elephants are evolutionary cousins of the woolly mammoth.

14
00:01:10.556 --> 00:01:15.179
The most obvious difference between these distant cousins is, of course, hair.

15
00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:21.524
Shaggy fur, which could grow up to fifty centimeters long, kept the woolly mammoth warm.

16
00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:27.524
Elephants have hair as well, but it is short and not thick enough to cover their hides.

17
00:01:28.085 --> 00:01:31.768
The prehistoric woolly mammoth grew larger than today's elephants.

18
00:01:32.528 --> 00:01:39.114
While archaeological evidence shows that mammoths grew up to four and a half meters high, the largest African

19
00:01:39.154 --> 00:01:41.295
elephants can only reach four meters.

20
00:01:42.176 --> 00:01:45.599
Like their elephant cousins, woolly mammoths had tusks.

21
00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:51.604
When two elephants in a herd challenge each other, they meet head to head and lock tusks.

22
00:01:52.264 --> 00:01:54.525
Mammoths probably wrestled in the same way.

23
00:01:55.306 --> 00:02:02.030
But the woolly mammoths' tusks were thicker than elephant tusks, and almost twice as long; some reached five

24
00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:02.950
meters in length.

25
00:02:03.911 --> 00:02:09.955
Another difference is that mammoth tusks grew in long, sweeping arcs that could reach the ground when the

26
00:02:10.015 --> 00:02:11.255
mammoth lowered its head.

27
00:02:12.236 --> 00:02:16.438
Mammoths probably lived in herds, as their elephant cousins do today.

28
00:02:16.919 --> 00:02:18.860
But the mammoth lived during an ice age.

29
00:02:19.648 --> 00:02:23.771
How did such a large animal survive in such an inhospitable environment?

30
00:02:24.411 --> 00:02:28.494
Woolly mammoths had more than a thick coat to protect them from the harsh weather.

31
00:02:29.214 --> 00:02:36.039
Underneath their hides woolly mammoths had a layer of fat as thick as eight centimeters, which insulated them

32
00:02:36.099 --> 00:02:36.839
from the cold.

33
00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:43.404
The woolly mammoth's cold environment also explains why elephants and mammoths have such different ears.

34
00:02:44.216 --> 00:02:49.438
African and Indian elephants have large ears, sometimes two square meters in size.

35
00:02:50.078 --> 00:02:56.120
During the hot summer, an African elephant fans its ears, sending a cool breeze over its body.

36
00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:02.422
Ears are full of tiny blood vessels close to the skin's surface, and waving its large, thin ears

37
00:03:03.002 --> 00:03:05.663
also helps an elephant release heat from its body.

38
00:03:06.423 --> 00:03:09.904
Of course, mammoths had to face cold weather, not hot.

39
00:03:10.792 --> 00:03:16.654
Their ears evolved into a much smaller size so that their bodies would retain heat and not lose

40
00:03:16.714 --> 00:03:17.754
it to the cold air.

41
00:03:18.795 --> 00:03:25.417
Woolly mammoths were herbivores, and finding food in the snow-covered plains of the last ice age was quite

42
00:03:25.477 --> 00:03:32.599
difficult. But even today large herbivores, like the reindeer and the yak, live in great numbers in northern

43
00:03:32.639 --> 00:03:36.200
climates. Vegetation can be found under the snow.

44
00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:43.005
Many scientists believe that mammoths made good use of their tusks, swinging them from side to side to

45
00:03:43.065 --> 00:03:45.927
clear away snow and expose the plants underneath.

46
00:03:46.768 --> 00:03:50.611
The mammoth's long, curved tusks seem suited to this purpose.

47
00:03:51.472 --> 00:03:57.657
Many types of nutritious plant life that are extinct today grew during the last ice age, so the

48
00:03:57.697 --> 00:03:59.958
woolly mammoth would have had enough to feed on.

49
00:04:00.779 --> 00:04:08.145
Also mammoth herds likely traveled across the vast North to find the best feeding areas, as reindeer do

50
00:04:08.205 --> 00:04:13.388
today. The woolly mammoth's environment was very different from the one we know today.

51
00:04:14.149 --> 00:04:22.434
During the Pleistocene, giant sloths as tall as elephants, relatives of today's slow-moving tree sloths, walked the earth.

52
00:04:23.395 --> 00:04:28.659
The strange glyptodon, with its turtle-like shell, was as big as a car.

53
00:04:29.479 --> 00:04:33.602
Saber-toothed tigers and giant bears were dangerous predators.

54
00:04:34.422 --> 00:04:39.569
As the last ice age came to an end, most of these large animals became extinct.

55
00:04:40.349 --> 00:04:47.751
The giant sloth, woolly rhinoceros, cave lion, saber-toothed tiger, and many others all died out.

56
00:04:48.391 --> 00:04:50.592
This was also the end of the woolly mammoth.

57
00:04:51.512 --> 00:04:58.554
For many years scientists suspected that humankind's ancestors were responsible for hunting these animals to extinction.

58
00:04:59.395 --> 00:05:03.536
But scientists now believe that climate change was also partly responsible.

59
00:05:04.372 --> 00:05:09.274
The warming temperatures at the end of the last ice age led to a change in the species

60
00:05:09.314 --> 00:05:10.975
of plants available for food.

61
00:05:11.815 --> 00:05:18.458
The combination of rising temperatures and different plants was too difficult for the giant herbivores to adapt to.

62
00:05:19.299 --> 00:05:22.160
There was one notable and strange exception, however.

63
00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:30.223
Some woolly mammoths survived on the small and remote Wrangel Island in the Russian Arctic until approximately 1700

64
00:05:30.263 --> 00:05:38.160
BC. The island was too small to support a large number of full-size mammoths, but a population of

65
00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:40.162
dwarf mammoths survived there.

66
00:05:40.963 --> 00:05:47.047
To this day Wrangel Island supports a greater number of Arctic plants than any other place on the

67
00:05:47.107 --> 00:05:48.669
planet of similar size.

68
00:05:49.569 --> 00:05:55.914
It's intriguing to think that in 2000 BC, when the great pyramids were being built in Egypt, woolly

69
00:05:55.954 --> 00:05:57.996
mammoths still wandered the plains.

70
00:05:59.176 --> 00:06:05.061
Most of what we know about mammoths comes from the study of frozen carcasses found in northern Siberia.

71
00:06:05.861 --> 00:06:12.166
Only a few dozen mammoth remains have been found, and bodies that are complete are very rare.

72
00:06:12.887 --> 00:06:19.932
But unlike the fossil and skeleton remains of other extinct species, the frozen remains of mammoths allow scientists

73
00:06:19.952 --> 00:06:24.576
to study their skin, flesh, and sometimes their internal organs.

74
00:06:25.456 --> 00:06:29.780
Some scientists are investigating ways to bring the woolly mammoth back to life.

75
00:06:30.641 --> 00:06:37.867
One proposal involves extracting frozen sperm from a preserved mammoth's reproductive organs, and using it to fertilize the

76
00:06:37.967 --> 00:06:39.628
egg of a female elephant.

77
00:06:40.469 --> 00:06:42.711
Another method would be to clone the animal.

78
00:06:43.532 --> 00:06:49.857
Scientists would have to extract DNA from a piece of a preserved mammoth carcass, then replace the DNA

79
00:06:50.017 --> 00:06:52.900
of an elephant's egg with the mammoth's DNA.

80
00:06:53.688 --> 00:06:59.914
Scientists would stimulate the egg to start growing and place it inside a female elephant for the duration

81
00:06:59.954 --> 00:07:01.275
of the gestation period.

82
00:07:02.236 --> 00:07:04.398
There are problems with both of these techniques.

83
00:07:05.258 --> 00:07:10.963
Even under laboratory conditions, it is hard for egg and sperm cells to survive freezing.

84
00:07:11.704 --> 00:07:18.189
Furthermore, if intact sperm cells from a woolly mammoth could be found, using them to fertilize an elephant

85
00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:20.852
egg would not bring the mammoth back to life.

86
00:07:21.764 --> 00:07:26.967
Instead it would create an animal that is a hybrid of the woolly mammoth and the elephant.

87
00:07:27.747 --> 00:07:29.708
Cloning is a more promising method.

88
00:07:30.509 --> 00:07:37.313
For many years the idea of cloning a mammoth seemed ridiculous because DNA from a frozen carcass is

89
00:07:37.373 --> 00:07:38.573
typically fragmented.

90
00:07:39.394 --> 00:07:47.798
But recently geneticists have made progress analyzing fragmented DNA, and a significant portion of mammoth DNA has been

91
00:07:47.878 --> 00:07:55.663
reassembled. It may take many years, but one day scientists may clone the long-dead cousin to the elephant,

92
00:07:56.204 --> 00:07:59.108
and the woolly mammoth will walk the earth again.
